eau"},{"type":"add","old":null,"new":9,"text":"@author Sam Brannen"},{"type":"add","old":null,"new":10,"text":"@author Scott Andrews"},{"type":"add","old":null,"new":11,"text":""},{"type":"add","old":null,"new":12,"text":"=========================================================================="},{"type":"add","old":null,"new":13,"text":"=== Data Access Strategies"},{"type":"add","old":null,"new":14,"text":"=========================================================================="},{"type":"add","old":null,"new":15,"text":""},{"type":"add","old":null,"new":16,"text":"PetClinic features alternative DAO implementations and application"},{"type":"add","old":null,"new":17,"text":"configurations for JDBC, Hibernate, and JPA, with HSQLDB and MySQL as"},{"type":"add","old":null,"new":18,"text":"target databases. The default PetClinic configuration is JDBC on HSQLDB."},{"type":"add","old":null,"new":19,"text":"See \"src/main/resources/jdbc.properties\" as well as web.xml and"},{"type":"add","old":null,"new":20,"text":"applicationContext-*.xml in the \"src/main/webapp/WEB-INF\" folder for"},{"type":"add","old":null,"new":21,"text":"details. A simple comment change in web.xml switches between the data"},{"type":"add","old":null,"new":22,"text":"access strategies."},{"type":"add","old":null,"new":23,"text":""},{"type":"add","old":null,"new":24,"text":"The JDBC and Hibernate versions of PetClinic also demonstrate JMX support"},{"type":"add","old":null,"new":25,"text":"via the use of <context:mbean-export/> for exporting MBeans."},{"type":"add","old":null,"new":26,"text":"SimpleJdbcClinic exposes the SimpleJdbcClinicMBean management interface"},{"type":"add","old":null,"new":27,"text":"via JMX through the use of the @ManagedResource and @ManagedOperation"},{"type":"add","old":null,"new":28,"text":"annotations; whereas, the HibernateStatistics service is exposed via JMX"},{"type":"add","old":null,"new":29,"text":"through auto-detection of the service MBean. You can start up the JDK's"},{"type":"add","old":null,"new":30,"text":"JConsole to manage the exported bean."},{"type":"add","old":null,"new":31,"text":""},{"type":"add","old":null,"new":32,"text":"All data access strategies can work with JTA for transaction management by"},{"type":"add","old":null,"new":33,"text":"activating the JtaTransactionManager and a JndiObjectFactoryBean that"},{"type":"add","old":null,"new":34,"text":"refers to a transactional container DataSource. The default for JDBC is"},{"type":"add","old":null,"new":35,"text":"DataSourceTransactionManager; for Hibernate, HibernateTransactionManager;"},{"type":"add","old":null,"new":36,"text":"for JPA, JpaTransactionManager. Those local strategies allow for working"},{"type":"add","old":null,"new":37,"text":"with any locally defined DataSource."},{"type":"add","old":null,"new":38,"text":""},{"type":"add","old":null,"new":39,"text":"Note that the sample configurations for JDBC, Hibernate, and JPA configure"},{"type":"add","old":null,"new":40,"text":"a BasicDataSource from the Apache Commons DBCP project for connection"},{"type":"add","old":null,"new":41,"text":"pooling."},{"type":"add","old":null,"new":42,"text":""},{"type":"add","old":null,"new":43,"text":"=========================================================================="},{"type":"add","old":null,"new":44,"text":"=== Build and Deployment"},{"type":"add","old":null,"new":45,"text":"=========================================================================="},{"type":"add","old":null,"new":46,"text":""},{"type":"add","old":null,"new":47,"text":"The Spring PetClinic sample application is built using Spring Build, which"},{"type":"add","old":null,"new":48,"text":"is a custom build solution based on Ant and Ivy for dependency management."},{"type":"add","old":null,"new":49,"text":"For deployment, the web application needs to be built with Apache Ant 1.6"},{"type":"add","old":null,"new":50,"text":"or higher. When the project is first built, Spring Build will use Ivy to"},{"type":"add","old":null,"new":51,"text":"automatically download all required dependencies. Thus the initial build"},{"type":"add","old":null,"new":52,"text":"may take a few minutes depending on the speed of your Internet connection,"},{"type":"add","old":null,"new":53,"text":"but subsequent builds will be much faster."},{"type":"add","old":null,"new":54,"text":""},{"type":"add","old":null,"new":55,"text":"Available build commands:"},{"type":"add","old":null,"new":56,"text":""},{"type":"add","old":null,"new":57,"text":"- ant clean        --> cleans the project"},{"type":"add","old":null,"new":58,"text":"- ant clean test   --> cleans the project and runs all tests"},{"type":"add","old":null,"new":59,"text":"- ant clean jar    --> cleans the project and builds the WAR"},{"type":"add","old":null,"new":60,"text":""},{"type":"add","old":null,"new":61,"text":"After building the project with \"ant clean jar\", you will find the"},{"type":"add","old":null,"new":62,"text":"resulting WAR file in the \"target/artifacts\" directory. By default, an"},{"type":"add","old":null,"new":63,"text":"embedded HSQLDB instance in configured. No other steps are necessary to"},{"type":"add","old":null,"new":64,"text":"get the data source up and running: you can simply deploy the built WAR"},{"type":"add","old":null,"new":65,"text":"file directly to your Servlet container."},{"type":"add","old":null,"new":66,"text":""},{"type":"add","old":null,"new":67,"text":"For MySQL, you'll need to use the corresponding schema and SQL scripts in"},{"type":"add","old":null,"new":68,"text":"the \"db/mysql\" subdirectory. Follow the steps outlined in"}]}],"18.2":[{"path":"readme.txt","oldPath":"readme.txt","binary":false,"renameOnly":false,"rows":[{"type":"add","old":null,"new":69,"text":"\"db/mysql/petclinic_db_setup_mysql.txt\" for explicit details."},{"type":"add","old":null,"new":70,"text":""},{"type":"add","old":null,"new":71,"text":"In you intend to use a local DataSource, the JDBC settings can be adapted"},{"type":"add","old":null,"new":72,"text":"in \"src/main/resources/jdbc.properties\". To use a JTA DataSource, you need"},{"type":"add","old":null,"new":73,"text":"to set up corresponding DataSources in your Java EE container."},{"type":"add","old":null,"new":74,"text":""},{"type":"add","old":null,"new":75,"text":"Notes on enabling Log4J:"},{"type":"add","old":null,"new":76,"text":" - Log4J is disabled by default due to issues with JBoss."},{"type":"add","old":null,"new":77,"text":" - Uncomment the Log4J listener in \"WEB-INF/web.xml\" to enable logging."},{"type":"add","old":null,"new":78,"text":""},{"type":"add","old":null,"new":79,"text":"=========================================================================="},{"type":"add","old":null,"new":80,"text":"=== JPA on Tomcat"},{"type":"add","old":null,"new":81,"text":"=========================================================================="},{"type":"add","old":null,"new":82,"text":""},{"type":"add","old":null,"new":83,"text":"This section provides tips on using the Java Persistence API (JPA) on"},{"type":"add","old":null,"new":84,"text":"Apache Tomcat 4.x or higher with a persistence provider that requires"},{"type":"add","old":null,"new":85,"text":"class instrumentation (such as TopLink Essentials)."},{"type":"add","old":null,"new":86,"text":""},{"type":"add","old":null,"new":87,"text":"To use JPA class instrumentation, Tomcat has to be instructed to use a"},{"type":"add","old":null,"new":88,"text":"custom class loader which supports instrumentation. See the JPA section of"},{"type":"add","old":null,"new":89,"text":"the Spring reference manual for complete details."},{"type":"add","old":null,"new":90,"text":""},{"type":"add","old":null,"new":91,"text":"The basic steps are:"},{"type":"add","old":null,"new":92,"text":" - Copy \"org.springframework.instrument.tomcat-3.0.0.RELEASE.jar\" from the"},{"type":"add","old":null,"new":93,"text":"   Spring distribution to \"TOMCAT_HOME/server/lib\"."},{"type":"add","old":null,"new":94,"text":" - If you're running on Tomcat 5.x, modify \"TOMCAT_HOME/conf/server.xml\""},{"type":"add","old":null,"new":95,"text":"   and add a new \"<Context>\" element for 'petclinic' (see below). You can "},{"type":"add","old":null,"new":96,"text":"   alternatively deploy the WAR including \"META-INF/context.xml\" from this "},{"type":"add","old":null,"new":97,"text":"   sample application's \"src/main/webapp\" directory, in which case you"},{"type":"add","old":null,"new":98,"text":"   will need to uncomment the Loader element in that file to enable the"},{"type":"add","old":null,"new":99,"text":"   use of the TomcatInstrumentableClassLoader."},{"type":"add","old":null,"new":100,"text":""},{"type":"add","old":null,"new":101,"text":"<Context path=\"/petclinic\" docBase=\"/petclinic/location\" ...>"},{"type":"add","old":null,"new":102,"text":"  <!-- please note that useSystemClassLoaderAsParent is available since Tomcat 5.5.20; remove it if previous versions are being used -->"},{"type":"add","old":null,"new":103,"text":"  <Loader loaderClass=\"org.springframework.instrument.classloading.tomcat.TomcatInstrumentableClassLoader\" useSystemClassLoaderAsParent=\"false\"/>"},{"type":"add","old":null,"new":104,"text":"  ..."},{"type":"add","old":null,"new":105,"text":"</Context>"}]}],"18.3":[{"path":".classpath","oldPath":".classpath","binary":false,"renameOnly":false,"rows":[{"type":"add","old":null,"new":1,"text":"<?xml version=\"1.0\" encoding=\"UTF-8\"?>"},{"type":"add","old":null,"new":2,"text":"<classpath>"},{"type":"add","old":null,"new":3,"text":"\t<classpathentry kind=\"src\" path=\"src/main/java\"/>"},{"type":"add","old":null,"new":4,"text":"\t<classpathentry kind=\"src\" path=\"src/main/resources\"/>"},{"type":"add","old":null,"new":5,"text":"\t<classpathentry kind=\"src\" output=\"target/test-classes\" path=\"src/test/java\"/>"},{"type":"add","old":null,"new":6,"text":"\t<classpathentry kind=\"src\" output=\"target/test-classes\" path=\"src/te